    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [3, 6, 9, 1]          ->  3   (sorted 1, 3, 6, 9: every gap is 3)\nnums = [1, 10, 5, 50, 52, 25] ->  25  (sorted 1, 5, 10, 25, 50, 52)\nnums = [10]                   ->  0"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: the pigeonhole principle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Sorting and scanning neighbours is O(n log n) — too slow for the requirement. But we don't need the full order, only the **largest** gap. With n values between `lo` and `hi`, there are n − 1 gaps adding up to `hi − lo`, so the largest is **at least the average**, (hi − lo) / (n − 1)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "So cut the range into **buckets narrower than that**. Two values in the same bucket are closer than the average gap, so the maximum gap never lies inside a bucket — it always runs from the **largest value of one non-empty bucket** to the **smallest value of the next non-empty one**. Each bucket only needs its min and max, and one scan over the buckets finds the answer: linear time, no sorting."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Sort, then scan neighbours"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function maximumGap(nums) {\n  const a = [...nums].sort((x, y) => x - y);\n  let best = 0;\n  for (let i = 1; i < a.length; i++) best = Math.max(best, a[i] - a[i - 1]);\n  return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def maximum_gap(nums):\n    a = sorted(nums)\n    return max((a[i] - a[i - 1] for i in range(1, len(a))), default=0)"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int maximumGap(int[] nums) {\n    int[] a = nums.clone();\n    Arrays.sort(a);\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) best = Math.max(best, a[i] - a[i - 1]);\n    return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int maximumGap(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> a = nums;\n    sort(a.begin(), a.end());\n    int best = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 1; i < a.size(); i++) best = max(best, a[i] - a[i - 1]);\n    return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n log n), space O(n).** Correct, but it breaks the linear-time requirement. (Radix sort would make this approach linear for 32-bit integers too.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Buckets"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "maximum-gap",
      "caption": "Drop each value into a bucket narrower than the average gap, then compare neighbouring non-empty buckets."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function maximumGap(nums) {\n  const n = nums.length;\n  if (n < 2) return 0;\n  const lo = Math.min(...nums), hi = Math.max(...nums);\n  if (lo === hi) return 0;\n  const size = Math.max(1, Math.floor((hi - lo) / (n - 1)));   // the biggest gap is at least this wide\n  const count = Math.floor((hi - lo) / size) + 1;\n  const bucketMin = new Array(count).fill(Infinity);\n  const bucketMax = new Array(count).fill(-Infinity);\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    const b = Math.floor((x - lo) / size);\n    bucketMin[b] = Math.min(bucketMin[b], x);\n    bucketMax[b] = Math.max(bucketMax[b], x);\n  }\n  let best = 0, prevMax = lo;\n  for (let b = 0; b < count; b++) {\n    if (bucketMin[b] === Infinity) continue;          // empty bucket\n    best = Math.max(best, bucketMin[b] - prevMax);    // gaps inside a bucket are never the biggest\n    prevMax = bucketMax[b];\n  }\n  return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def maximum_gap(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n < 2:\n        return 0\n    lo, hi = min(nums), max(nums)\n    if lo == hi:\n        return 0\n    size = max(1, (hi - lo) // (n - 1))   # the biggest gap is at least this wide\n    count = (hi - lo) // size + 1\n    bucket_min = [None] * count\n    bucket_max = [None] * count\n    for x in nums:\n        b = (x - lo) // size\n        bucket_min[b] = x if bucket_min[b] is None else min(bucket_min[b], x)\n        bucket_max[b] = x if bucket_max[b] is None else max(bucket_max[b], x)\n    best, prev_max = 0, lo\n    for b in range(count):\n        if bucket_min[b] is None:\n            continue                      # empty bucket\n        best = max(best, bucket_min[b] - prev_max)   # gaps inside a bucket are never the biggest\n        prev_max = bucket_max[b]\n    return best"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int maximumGap(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    if (n < 2) return 0;\n    int lo = Integer.MAX_VALUE, hi = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n    for (int x : nums) { lo = Math.min(lo, x); hi = Math.max(hi, x); }\n    if (lo == hi) return 0;\n    int size = Math.max(1, (int) (((long) hi - lo) / (n - 1)));   // the biggest gap is at least this wide\n    int count = (int) (((long) hi - lo) / size) + 1;\n    int[] bucketMin = new int[count], bucketMax = new int[count];\n    Arrays.fill(bucketMin, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n    Arrays.fill(bucketMax, Integer.MIN_VALUE);\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        int b = (int) (((long) x - lo) / size);\n        bucketMin[b] = Math.min(bucketMin[b], x);\n        bucketMax[b] = Math.max(bucketMax[b], x);\n    }\n    int best = 0, prevMax = lo;\n    for (int b = 0; b < count; b++) {\n        if (bucketMin[b] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) continue;   // empty bucket\n        best = Math.max(best, bucketMin[b] - prevMax);     // gaps inside a bucket are never the biggest\n        prevMax = bucketMax[b];\n    }\n    return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int maximumGap(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    if (n < 2) return 0;\n    int lo = *min_element(nums.begin(), nums.end()), hi = *max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    if (lo == hi) return 0;\n    long long size = max(1LL, ((long long)hi - lo) / (n - 1));   // the biggest gap is at least this wide\n    int count = ((long long)hi - lo) / size + 1;\n    vector<int> bucketMin(count, INT_MAX), bucketMax(count, INT_MIN);\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        int b = ((long long)x - lo) / size;\n        bucketMin[b] = min(bucketMin[b], x);\n        bucketMax[b] = max(bucketMax[b], x);\n    }\n    int best = 0, prevMax = lo;\n    for (int b = 0; b < count; b++) {\n        if (bucketMin[b] == INT_MAX) continue;            // empty bucket\n        best = max(best, bucketMin[b] - prevMax);         // gaps inside a bucket are never the biggest\n        prevMax = bucketMax[b];\n    }\n    return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(n).** There are about n buckets; filling them and scanning them are both linear. Why bucket width `⌊(hi − lo)/(n − 1)⌋` is safe: two values in one bucket differ by at most width − 1, which is less than the average gap and so less than the maximum gap."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Sort, then scan neighbours",
          "time": "O(n log n)",
          "timeWhy": "The **sort** dominates; the neighbour scan is O(n).",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The sorted copy."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Buckets",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "About **n** buckets; filling them and scanning their min and max are both linear.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "A min and max **per bucket**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This is the hardest question in the chapter because the trick is a counting argument, not code: once you see that the maximum gap is at least the average gap, the buckets follow."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Fewer than two values** — 0.",
        "**All values equal** — 0 (and avoid dividing by zero for the bucket width).",
        "**Two values** — their difference.",
        "**Large values** — near 10⁹ in Java or C++, compute `hi - lo` in a 64-bit type."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Contains Duplicate III** — buckets of width t + 1 find values within t of each other in O(n).",
        "**Radix sort** — the other linear-time route for integers (see Counting sort and radix sort).",
        "**Minimum Absolute Difference** — the smallest neighbouring gap instead of the largest. Pattern 1."
      ]
    }
  ],
